An antiaircraft gun fires at an elevation of $60^{\circ}

$ at an enemy aircraft at $10,000\ \mathrm{m}

$ above the ground. At what speed must the cannon be shot to hit the plane at that height?

Balance the redox reaction.

$\text{C}{{\text{u}}^{\text{+}}}\text{(aq)+Fe(s)}\to \text{F}{{\text{e}}^{\text{3+}}}\text{(aq)+Cu(s)}

$

For a touring bicyclist the drag coefficient $C

$(where $f_{air} = 12 CA \rho v^{2}

$) is 1.09, the frontal area is 0.461 m$^{2}

$, the air density $\rho

$ is 1.20 kg/m$^{3}

$, & the coefficient of rolling friction is 4.80 x 10$^{-3}

$. The rider has mass 59.0 kg, and her bike has mass 11.7 kg.

For racing, the same rider uses a different bike with a coefficient of rolling friction 3.30 x 10$^{-3}

$ and mass 8.90 kg. She also crouches down, reducing her drag coefficient to 0.860 and reducing her frontal area to 0.362 m$^{2}

$.

What must her power output to the rear wheel be then to maintain a speed of 11.3 m/s?

A human has $10^{14}

$ cells and each human cell has about $6.4\times 10^{9}

$ nucleotide pairs of DNA. What is the length of a double helix that could be formed from this amount of DNA in a human individual?

Particle 1 has a mass ${{\text{m}}_{\text{1}}}\text{ = 1}\text{.11 g}

$ and particle 2 has a mass ${{\text{m}}_{\text{2}}}\text{ = 1}\text{.98 g}

$. They head towards each other along the x-axis moving in opposite directions for a head-on collision. Particle 1 comes in with a speed of ${{\text{v}}_{\text{1}}}\text{ = 5}\text{.09 m/s}

$ moving to the right. Particle 2 is moving to the left heading towards particle 1 with the same magnitude of momentum as particle 1. The collision is perfectly elastic. What is the initial speed of particle 2?
